WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.720
In the rivers of the Amazon&nbsp;
rainforest is a fish like no other.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.920
A fish that you would not want to find&nbsp;
yourself wading in the water with.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.960
A specimen of this fish was brought to&nbsp;
Philadelphia from South America in 1773.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.720
It was observed that:

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:26.960
It had the extraordinary power of communicating&nbsp;
a painful sensation, like that of an electrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.440
shock, to people who touched it, and&nbsp;
of killing its prey at a distance.”

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:39.120
It was a fish so unusual, that it would go on&nbsp;
to change the course of science - and in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.600
the course of modern society - forever.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.200
Electric eels are a genus of&nbsp;
freshwater fish from South America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:53.920
famous for their ability to stun their prey by&nbsp;
generating electricity, “Eel” is a misnomer, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:59.200
they are actually part of the knife fish family,&nbsp;
and are more closely related to catfish than eels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.680
And though they aren’t the only electric fish in&nbsp;
the world, they are by far, the most powerful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:12.800
Some individuals are able to deliver&nbsp;
shocks at up to 860 volts and 1 amp.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.920
The average shock from an electric eel&nbsp;
lasts about two thousandths of a second.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.920
A single shock likely wouldn’t&nbsp;
kill a person, but it could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.960
seriously stun one. If shocked,&nbsp;
your muscles would briefly contract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.560
and then you’d feel numb. If you were in the water&nbsp;
and got shocked, this could lead to drowning.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.160
It's one of only a few animals that can&nbsp;
kill its prey at a distance. And not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:40.240
only can the eel use electricity to stun or&nbsp;
kill its prey - it can also use electricity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:46.240
to control its target remotely - the&nbsp;
electricity coursing through the prey’s body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.040
making it act in a way detrimental to&nbsp;
itself, and beneficial for the eel.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.840
And as formidable a hunter as an electric eel is&nbsp;
on its own, recent studies have discovered that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:04.080
they are not the solitary predators researchers&nbsp;
always believed them to be. They are in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:10.160
proficient and effective pack hunters,&nbsp;
ambushing schools of fish in terrifying unison.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.400
How is it possible for a fish to generate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:21.840
so much electricity? And what has modern&nbsp;
science learned from this improbable creature?

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:35.520
Electric eels live in streams, floodplains, and&nbsp;
swamps of the Amazon and Orinoco river basins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.906
in South America. Such murky habitats can be&nbsp;
dangerously seasonal for a fish. During the dry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.906 --> 00:02:43.520
season, water levels drop, and oxygen availability&nbsp;
can plummet. But luckily for the electric eels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:49.920
they can breathe air, thanks to a special mucous&nbsp;
membrane in their mouth that can absorb oxygen.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:54.080
Electric eels are snake-like in&nbsp;
appearance, and they are pretty huge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.280
They can grow up to 8 feet,&nbsp;
or 2.5 meters in length,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.428
and weigh as much as 44 pounds, or 20 kg. They&nbsp;
don’t have scales, and instead, have slimy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.428 --> 00:03:04.554
dark gray or brown skin on the top of their body&nbsp;
and a yellow-orange color on the bottom side.

00:03:04.555 --> 00:03:08.800
And 80% of the eel's body,&nbsp;
by length and by weight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.400
is dedicated to one thing: generating&nbsp;
ridiculous amounts of electricity.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.520
The electric eel has three pairs&nbsp;
of electric organs that run through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:25.360
the back portion of the its body: the Sach’s&nbsp;
organ, the Hunter’s organ and the Main organ.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:33.040
The Main and Hunters' organs are the high voltage&nbsp;
producers, used for protection and stunning prey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.960
The Sachs' organ is capable only of producing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:42.480
low voltage pulses - its purpose is mainly&nbsp;
electro communication and navigation.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.240
These organs contain hundreds of&nbsp;
thousands of modified muscle cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.840
called electrocytes. These are flattened&nbsp;
disk-like cells that are stacked in about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.280
70 columns on each side of the fish’s body.&nbsp;
Each column contains 5000–10 000 electrocytes.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.920
In their resting state, these&nbsp;
electrocytes maintain a negative charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.280
as the cells constantly work to pump positively&nbsp;
charged sodium and potassium ions out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.840
And each electrocyte is connected to the&nbsp;
nervous system - but on only one side.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:20.880
When the eel senses prey or a threat, it sends a&nbsp;
signal through the nerve endings causing an ion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:27.840
channel on the electrocyte to open. Extracellular&nbsp;
positive ions can then flow back into the cell.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.440
The side without the nerve connection continues&nbsp;
to pump the positive ions out of the cell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.480
This gives the left side of the&nbsp;
cell a relatively positive charge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.320
and the right side a relatively negative charge.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:49.440
Voila, a dipole has been created, and the eel&nbsp;
can start zappin’. The electric charge flows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.960
towards the eel’s head, which is the more&nbsp;
positively charged side of its body, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.920
is then discharged through the water towards prey.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.760
And the more cells that are stacked and lined&nbsp;
up, the more electricity they can generate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:06.800
Because incredibly, the eel is able to open all&nbsp;
of its membrane gates at exactly the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.160
creating a huge jolt of electricity.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:15.920
This seems like an extreme ability, but it's&nbsp;
an ability based on what typical muscle and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:22.080
nerve cells already do. All muscle and&nbsp;
nerve cells have electrical potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.720
and a simple contraction of a muscle&nbsp;
will release a small amount of voltage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.440
The cells of an electric eel have&nbsp;
simply amplified that potential.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:39.200
An eel’s ability to make electricity is often&nbsp;
compared to a battery - and this comparison is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:46.800
no coincidence. The very first battery, invented&nbsp;
in 1800, was inspired by the electric eel.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:54.240
Italian physicist Alessandro Volta observed that&nbsp;
electric eels had stacks of cells that looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:06:00.480
like a roll of coins. He figured if he could&nbsp;
mimic this arrangement, he too, might be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:07.520
to generate electricity. He cut coin-shaped disks&nbsp;
from various “dissimilar metals” and stacked them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.280
trying to find any combination of&nbsp;
them that would create electricity.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:17.760
Eventually, he tried copper and zinc disks,&nbsp;
and separated the stacked pairs with saltwater.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.760
And lo and behold - electricity started&nbsp;
flowing. This came to be known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.560
voltaic stack - and is considered to be&nbsp;
the first electric battery ever made.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:33.040
Before this discovery,&nbsp;
scientists barely understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:38.960
static electricity. And the electric eel&nbsp;
was only beginning to reveal its mysteries.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.280
Scientists soon realized that eels don’t have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:48.560
one-zap-fits-all policy. They instead&nbsp;
are able to use different charges for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:54.000
different things - some for sensing their&nbsp;
environment, some for communicating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:01.840
some for stunning their prey, and even some&nbsp;
for controlling the movements of their prey.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:08.720
Electric eels can control&nbsp;
their electricity with finesse.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.720
In their dark and murky habitat, electric&nbsp;
eels can’t rely on eyesight for navigation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:18.000
In fact, their eyesight is extremely poor.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.800
They instead use weak electric discharges,&nbsp;
around 10 volts, to electrolocate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:31.360
This weak charge emanates from their body, and&nbsp;
the eel can detect distortions in the electric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:37.040
field from objects either conducting or&nbsp;
resisting their electricity. This weak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:42.880
electric discharge is created when the eel simply&nbsp;
activates only a subset of it’s electrocytes.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.480
Electric eels can even communicate&nbsp;
using low voltage electric pulses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:54.320
By varing the frequency that they produce&nbsp;
these pulses, researchers believe that eels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:58.560
can convey information about their&nbsp;
sex and reproductive receptivity.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:02.960
And, of course, eels use&nbsp;
their electricity for hunting.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:10.000
Electric eels are mostly nocturnal. They will wait&nbsp;
for darkness, and then sneak up on their prey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:14.000
and unleash a series of high&nbsp;
frequency, high voltage pulses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:19.280
In this video, the red frames indicate&nbsp;
when the electric organs are discharging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:24.920
After just a few milliseconds the&nbsp;
prey is completely immobilized.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:33.120
Such a high frequency, high voltage attack induces&nbsp;
massive whole-body muscle contractions in the prey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:40.000
fish. The electric shock causes the prey fish’s&nbsp;
motor neurons, and thus their muscles, to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:46.880
simultaneously activated. The higher the voltage,&nbsp;
the more intense the muscle contraction - and at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:53.040
600 volts or more, these attacks often cause&nbsp;
the the maximum possible muscle contraction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:59.360
causing the animal to completely seize&nbsp;
up. This is similar to how a TASER works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:06.000
Then, once it is immobilized, the eel can&nbsp;
feast. Electric eels don’t have upper teeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.480
so they rely so on suction to pull the prey&nbsp;
into their mouth before swallowing them whole.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:17.760
For a long time, scientists thought that eels&nbsp;
implemented these two types of discharge - low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:23.600
voltage for navigation and communication; high&nbsp;
voltage for immobilizing prey. But, they soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:30.240
learned, there is more to the story. And that eels&nbsp;
use their electricity in even more cunning ways.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:36.800
When looking at the timing of the electric pulses,&nbsp;
researchers noticed an interesting pattern.

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:43.520
‘‘Introduction of prey into the aquarium&nbsp;
arouses the eel, causing it to swim around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:46.800
but often stopping in a&nbsp;
particular corner of the aquarium.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:54.640
During these stops, two high-voltage pulses&nbsp;
with an interval of about 2 ms are emitted.’’

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:59.840
After these two high voltage&nbsp;
pulses, the eel takes a pause,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:04.080
and then proceeds with its usual&nbsp;
high-voltage, high frequency attack volley.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:09.600
These two high voltage pulses are&nbsp;
known as doublets - and at first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:14.960
their purpose wasn’t fully understood.&nbsp;
Why let out two quick pulses initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:18.880
if the massive attack volley will be&nbsp;
more than enough to immobilize prey?

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.720
Scientists carefully observed the eels’ behavior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:29.360
and noticed a few things. 1) a massive attack&nbsp;
volley would almost always come after the doublet;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:36.000
and 2) the doublets themselves resulted in&nbsp;
a massive whole body twitch in nearby fish.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:40.880
Perhaps the purpose of these&nbsp;
doublets was not to stun the prey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:44.240
but to force the prey to&nbsp;
reveal its location to the eel.

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:48.320
To test this, scientists&nbsp;
set up a clever experiment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:54.640
On one side of an agar barrier they placed an&nbsp;
electric eel. On the other side of the barrier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:58.320
they placed a dead fish, and connected&nbsp;
it to a electrical stimulator.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:05.840
The fish was then sealed in a bag, and&nbsp;
electrically isolated from the eel. The scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:11.200
could electrically jolt the fish with their&nbsp;
stimulator, but the eel could not. The stimulator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:16.880
was set up so that when the eel emitted a doublet,&nbsp;
it would send a similar shock to the dead fish.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:20.560
When the eels emitted its doublet, and the dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:25.040
fish received the simulated doublet -&nbsp;
the eels immediately responded to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:28.720
twitch with a high-voltage volley&nbsp;
and strike toward the dead fish.

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:32.400
But when the investigators&nbsp;
turned off the stimulator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:38.320
and the eel emitted its doublet - no twitch was&nbsp;
produced in the dead fish, and the eel did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:44.560
attack. The eel would only attack the fish if&nbsp;
it first twitched in response to its doublet.

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:51.040
This study shows that electric eels&nbsp;
can indeed remotely control their prey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.241
forcing them to reveal their location. The&nbsp;
doublet allows the eel to ask the question:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.241 --> 00:11:53.760
any fish out there I can eat? The&nbsp;
prey have no choice but to respond.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:12:00.720
But what if an eel finds itself near an entire&nbsp;
shoal of fish - a shoal that seems particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:07.280
good at keeping its distance to avoid such&nbsp;
shocks? To deal with this, some electric eels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:13.520
display a remarkable behavior - a behavior&nbsp;
unprecedented in the world of electric fish.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:21.200
Social predation - where groups of&nbsp;
animals coordinate to hunt and kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:25.040
prey - is a tactic commonly&nbsp;
seen in mammals like wolves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:30.320
orcas, or lions. In fish, this&nbsp;
behavior is almost unheard of.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:36.720
So when scientists travelling through&nbsp;
the Amazon river noticed groups of eels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:42.160
sometimes over 100 of them, seemingly&nbsp;
rounding up and attacking prey together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:44.659
they almost could not believe their eyes. And&nbsp;
these weren’t just any of the electric eels -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.659 --> 00:12:45.440
they were volta eels the most powerful of any of&nbsp;
the electric eels, with shocks up to 860 volts.

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:50.320
At dawn and dusk, the scientists&nbsp;
noticed an increase in eel activity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:54.560
with many individuals leaving the murky&nbsp;
depths and swimming near the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.120 --> 00:13:01.120
appearing to congregate. These groups of eels&nbsp;
would then swim together towards a shallow hunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:07.760
area that contained thousands of small fish. These&nbsp;
groups of sometimes 100s of eels would then start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:13.280
to swim in circles, herding the fish into prey&nbsp;
balls, and pushing them to shallower water.

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:20.800
Then, between 2 to 10 individual eels would&nbsp;
launch a joint predatory strike. In this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:26.240
video you can recognize the attacking eels by&nbsp;
their synchronized sinusoidal body posture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:31.360
The fish hit with the electrical attack&nbsp;
spasm so hard that they jump out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:38.080
water - and then return to the surface stunned and&nbsp;
motionless. The eels then quickly eat the fish.

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:42.800
The eels would carry out this&nbsp;
attack sequence five to seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:48.240
times, and each time, a different subset of&nbsp;
eels would produce the electrical attack.

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:54.800
Although the researchers weren’t able to measure&nbsp;
the voltage of the coordinated electric attacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.800 --> 00:14:02.160
they estimate that 10 Volta’s electric eels&nbsp;
working together could create 8600 volts.

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:05.040
This hunting technique allows the eels to subdue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:09.280
huge quantities of prey that would&nbsp;
normally be too evasive to capture.

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:14.080
And it’s not just that these eels&nbsp;
were stuck together in a confined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:16.880
area, forcing them to feed&nbsp;
on the same groups of fish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:23.440
These eels were congregating from far and&nbsp;
wide, to repeatedly forage together over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:28.400
using communication in the form of low&nbsp;
voltage electric pulses and body posturing.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:33.360
It’s unknown if the eels have any&nbsp;
familial relationship to each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:39.040
like is common with many other pack hunters.&nbsp;
But scientists are working to find that out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:45.840
Only 9 other species of fish have ever been&nbsp;
observed to hunt together - and the electric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:51.920
eel’s tactics may be the most formidable. This&nbsp;
shows us that animals we think we understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:59.280
can have more than one survival strategy - that in&nbsp;
isolated regions and specific groups of animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:03.200
predator prey interactions may&nbsp;
not be at all what we expect.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:10.000
One lingering question that may have come to your&nbsp;
mind is - how do the eels not shock each other?

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:14.640
It’s believed that the electric organ&nbsp;
of an electric eel is padded with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:18.640
adipose tissue that insulates the&nbsp;
fish from its own electric shock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:22.000
But how do they prevent getting&nbsp;
shocked from other eels?

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:25.840
The likely answer is, that they don’t.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:32.800
Electric eels are probably pretty huge for exactly&nbsp;
this reason. They are much larger than their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:38.800
prey - such that an eel’s current will easily&nbsp;
incapacitate their target, but won’t do much to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:45.120
their larger bodies, similarly to how a single&nbsp;
electric eel shock to us would hurt for a sec,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:50.800
but not kill us. To an eel, getting shocked&nbsp;
is likely just part of doing business.

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:58.160
An eel’s entire world revolves around its&nbsp;
electricity. And largely thanks to the electric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:04.800
eel, so does ours. Next time you charge your phone&nbsp;
or stick some double As in your remote, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:11.280
thank the electric eel for its contribution to our&nbsp;
understanding of electricity. And we continue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:17.520
be inspired by them. Researchers are currently&nbsp;
working on an entirely new type of battery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:24.240
inspired by eels - a soft and flexible battery&nbsp;
that can power medical implants and soft robots.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:29.600
We are likely just beginning to&nbsp;
understand these incredible creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:34.560
with many more of their mysteries still&nbsp;
lurking in the muddy waters of the Amazon.

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:40.480
Understanding the electric eel has ignited&nbsp;
a curiosity in me about the electricity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:46.160
in the world all around us. Its nearly&nbsp;
impossible to NOT take it for granted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:50.080
I know I certainly don’t often think&nbsp;
about how the lights in my kitchen work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:55.120
how my computer stays charged, or how the&nbsp;
grid in Texas is teetering on a knife edge&nbsp;&nbsp;

